⭐ 3rd Question: Maximum XOR of Two Numbers in an Array

⚡ Not in your list
⚡ Very popular in Google / Amazon
⚡ Uses Bit Manipulation + Trie

🧩 Problem Statement

Given an integer array nums, return the maximum XOR of any two numbers in the array.

🔎 Example

Input:

nums = [3,10,5,25,2,8]


Output:

28


Explanation:

5 ^ 25 = 28   (Maximum)

🧠 Core Idea

XOR gives maximum value when:

Bits are as different as possible

So we:

Build a Binary Trie

Insert numbers bit by bit (31 to 0)

For each number, try to find opposite bit in trie

🔥 Why Use Trie?

For every bit:

If current bit = 1 → we want 0

If current bit = 0 → we want 1

To maximize XOR.

⏱ Time Complexity

O(N × 32)
≈ O(N)

Space: O(N × 32)

💻 Java Code
class Solution {

    static class TrieNode {
        TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[2];
    }

    public int findMaximumXOR(int[] nums) {

        TrieNode root = new TrieNode();

        // Step 1: Build Trie
        for (int num : nums) {
            insert(root, num);
        }

        int maxXOR = 0;

        // Step 2: Find Maximum XOR
        for (int num : nums) {
            maxXOR = Math.max(maxXOR, findMax(root, num));
        }

        return maxXOR;
    }

    private void insert(TrieNode root, int num) {
        TrieNode node = root;

        for (int i = 31; i >= 0; i--) {
            int bit = (num >> i) & 1;

            if (node.children[bit] == null) {
                node.children[bit] = new TrieNode();
            }

            node = node.children[bit];
        }
    }

    private int findMax(TrieNode root, int num) {
        TrieNode node = root;
        int maxNum = 0;

        for (int i = 31; i >= 0; i--) {

            int bit = (num >> i) & 1;

            // Prefer opposite bit
            if (node.children[1 - bit] != null) {
                maxNum |= (1 << i);
                node = node.children[1 - bit];
            } else {
                node = node.children[bit];
            }
        }

        return maxNum;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Solution sol = new Solution();
        int[] nums = {3, 10, 5, 25, 2, 8};
        System.out.println(sol.findMaximumXOR(nums));
    }
}

🔍 Line-by-Line Explanation
🔹 TrieNode
TrieNode[] children = new TrieNode[2];


Each node stores:

children[0] → bit 0

children[1] → bit 1

🔹 insert()
for (int i = 31; i >= 0; i--)


We check from MSB (Most Significant Bit) to LSB.

int bit = (num >> i) & 1;


Extract ith bit.

If node does not exist → create it.

🔹 findMax()

For each bit:

1️⃣ Extract bit
2️⃣ Check opposite bit exists
3️⃣ If yes:

maxNum |= (1 << i);


This sets ith bit in answer.

Else → go same direction.

🔎 Step Example

nums = [3,10,5,25]

Binary:

3   = 00011
10  = 01010
5   = 00101
25  = 11001


5 ^ 25:

00101
11001
-----
11100 = 28

